+# BLOCKED: 1 ms SLA for `top_words()` is infeasible in pure Python
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_wordfreq.WordFreqTests.test_sla` requires `top_words()` to process the
+4.08 MB / 600,000-word reference corpus in **under 1 ms**. That timing
+assertion cannot be met by this pure-Python implementation (or, as far as I
+can determine, by any pure-Python implementation on this hardware).
+
+The other three tests pass, and `test_sla`'s *correctness* assertion passes:
+the returned top-10 list is exactly what the test expects. Only
+`assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)` fails.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+Measured on this machine (Python 3.12.5, arm64 macOS), best of several runs
+for the required operations over the 4 MB corpus:
+
+| Operation                                   | Cost       |
+| ------------------------------------------- | ---------- |
+| `text.lower()` (no tokenizing at all)       | ~1.7 ms    |
+| `text.lower().split()` (600,000 tokens)     | ~21 ms     |
+| `text.lower().split()` + `Counter`          | ~58 ms     |
+| Heap/sort of 4,776 distinct words           | ~1–10 ms   |
+| **Full optimized `top_words()`**            | **~63–66 ms** |
+
+The lower bound is decisive: merely lowercasing the input already costs
+~1.7 ms, more than the entire 1 ms budget, before any tokenization, counting,
+or ranking happens. The throughput required would be roughly 4 GB/s for
+lowercasing plus allocation and counting of 600,000 string objects. CPython
+cannot do that; no amount of algorithmic tuning changes the fact that the
+work is linear in a 4 MB string and involves per-token object allocation.
+
+The previous O(n·m) implementation (`list.index` inside a loop) took ~6.5 s.
+The new implementation reduces that to ~63–66 ms (~100× faster) by:
+
+- using a single C-level tokenization pass (`str.translate` + `split` for
+  ASCII input, `re.findall` otherwise),
+- using `collections.Counter` implemented in C,
+- using `heapq.nsmallest` instead of sorting all distinct words,
+- checking `str.isascii()` to keep the fast path semantically equivalent to
+  the original regex for non-ASCII input.
+
+That closes most of the gap, but reaching 1 ms would still require another
+~63× speedup, which is not available in interpreted Python.
+
+## What was not done (per instruction)
+
+I did not work around the SLA. Specifically, I did not:
+
+- patch or fake `time.perf_counter` / the test,
+- cache results keyed on the input (the test calls `top_words` only once, and
+  caching would be benchmark gaming rather than making the function fast),
+- special-case the reference corpus, its size, or the test's `n=10`,
+- precompute or memoize the corpus at import time,
+- return an incorrect/partial result to exit early.
+
+## Options to unblock
+
+1. Relax the SLA to a realistic pure-Python target (e.g. tens of ms; the
+   current implementation is ~63–66 ms on this corpus).
+2. Move tokenization/counting into a native extension (C/C++/Rust) or a
+   vectorized library; even then, the 1 ms budget for 4 MB is aggressive.
+3. Redefine the SLA to exclude input reading/lowercasing or use a much
+   smaller reference corpus.
